WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.006
Aisha Coleman: Hi Monica, hi Stacy. Can you hear me okay?

2
00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:13.558
Monica Chen: We can. Good to hear from you, Aisha.

3
00:00:14.104 --> 00:00:22.932
Aisha Coleman: How's everything on the lending floor this week? I saw a slight dip in total calls last Tuesday but it bounced back.

4
00:00:23.552 --> 00:00:34.169
Monica Chen: Yeah, that was our own doing. We had a team training on the new ARM products that morning, so the floor was thin until about 11.

5
00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:42.876
Stacy Klein: Plus two of the loan officers were out. Reggie had a family thing and Tom was at a closing.

6
00:00:43.078 --> 00:00:49.617
Aisha Coleman: Makes sense. Volume looks healthy otherwise. You're pacing around 14,200 calls for September, which is up roughly 6% over August.

7
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:57.527
Monica Chen: That tracks. We added four loan officers last month too, so I'd expect that to keep climbing into Q4.

8
00:00:58.212 --> 00:01:01.936
Aisha Coleman: Good. And PreSense is still routing by intent without issues?

9
00:01:02.301 --> 00:01:04.018
Monica Chen: Fine. No complaints there.

10
00:01:04.1000 --> 00:01:09.683
Aisha Coleman: Great. Stacy, you had a couple of things you wanted to walk through?

11
00:01:10.321 --> 00:01:19.273
Stacy Klein: I do. Both are things I've been collecting since the summer, and I finally have enough examples to describe them properly.

12
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.380
Aisha Coleman: Go ahead.

13
00:01:22.539 --> 00:01:30.475
Stacy Klein: First one is on the Signal AI side. Because we're regulated, anything a transcript or an AI summary exposes has to be controlled.

14
00:01:30.475 --> 00:01:35.995
Stacy Klein: Right now the redaction we get is not configurable at the level our compliance team wants.

15
00:01:36.591 --> 00:01:39.953
Aisha Coleman: What do you mean when you say the level?

16
00:01:40.157 --> 00:02:03.534
Stacy Klein: We need to pick which identifiers get masked, and we need it driven by role. A loan officer shouldn't see a full Social Security number on a transcript, but someone in compliance reviewing a complaint should be able to unmask it, and that unmask action needs to land in an audit log showing who viewed what and when.

17
00:02:03.928 --> 00:02:09.592
Aisha Coleman: So configurable redaction plus role-based access plus an audit trail of transcript views.

18
00:02:10.275 --> 00:02:18.928
Stacy Klein: Exactly. Our examiner asked me in July how we'd prove who had seen a given transcript, and I did not have a good answer.

19
00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:31.115
Aisha Coleman: Okay. That's clear. I'll write it up with your compliance angle and take it to the product team. Can you send me one redacted example and one that should have been redacted but wasn't?

20
00:02:31.343 --> 00:02:36.135
Stacy Klein: I can. I'll pull two from September and send them this afternoon.

21
00:02:37.217 --> 00:02:39.315
Aisha Coleman: Perfect. What's the second one?

22
00:02:40.369 --> 00:02:49.864
Stacy Klein: The Salesforce integration. When we change a field on the Loan Application object, or add a picklist value, the call data stops writing to the custom object.

23
00:02:51.306 --> 00:02:53.928
Monica Chen: That's the one that bit us in August.

24
00:02:54.836 --> 00:03:06.926
Stacy Klein: Right. We added a 'Property Type' picklist value for condos, and calls just stopped landing on the object. No error on our side, nothing in the call record, just silence.

25
00:03:08.365 --> 00:03:10.357
Aisha Coleman: How did you catch it?

26
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:21.329
Stacy Klein: Reggie noticed his call notes weren't showing up on his applications. Took us about a day and a half to figure out it was the mapping.

27
00:03:21.329 --> 00:03:31.764
Stacy Klein: And once we refreshed the sandbox in September we had to rebuild the whole mapping from scratch, which is a lot of manual work for one person.

28
00:03:32.528 --> 00:03:37.016
Aisha Coleman: So the change itself and the sandbox refresh both break the mapping.

29
00:03:38.166 --> 00:03:38.966
Stacy Klein: Yes.

30
00:03:40.201 --> 00:03:45.043
Aisha Coleman: Does it recover on its own, or do you have to intervene every time?

31
00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.618
Stacy Klein: We intervene. Every time.

32
00:03:48.763 --> 00:03:56.145
Aisha Coleman: Okay. Send me the ticket numbers and the dates, and I'll attach them. Anything else broken, or is that it?

33
00:03:56.792 --> 00:03:57.592
Stacy Klein: That's it.

34
00:03:58.163 --> 00:04:07.475
Monica Chen: Quality Management is working fine for us, for what it's worth. We're scoring about 4,000 calls a month now, which is more than we did in the spring.

35
00:04:07.475 --> 00:04:11.134
Monica Chen: The loan officers grumble about it, but they grumble about everything.

36
00:04:11.593 --> 00:04:15.067
Aisha Coleman: Understood. Anything you want me to look at there?

37
00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:17.145
Monica Chen: No, we're good.

38
00:04:17.744 --> 00:04:31.416
Aisha Coleman: Then let me get both of these written up. I'll have the Signal AI item in front of the product team by Wednesday, and I'll keep you posted on what they say.

39
00:04:31.885 --> 00:04:36.413
Stacy Klein: That works. I'll send the examples and the ticket numbers today.

40
00:04:36.897 --> 00:04:49.362
Aisha Coleman: One more thing before we go. Your renewal is next April. Do you want to put a business review on the calendar for January, so we're not doing it at the last minute?

41
00:04:50.137 --> 00:04:53.102
Monica Chen: January is good. Maybe the second week?

42
00:04:54.488 --> 00:04:56.635
Aisha Coleman: I'll send a couple of options.

43
00:04:58.069 --> 00:04:58.869
Monica Chen: Thanks, Aisha.

44
00:04:59.387 --> 00:05:00.816
Aisha Coleman: Thanks both. Talk soon.
